  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "Suppose that $g(x)$ is a  function such that  ${\\int }_{4}^{8}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x=5$ and ${\\int }_{4}^{1}3g(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-6.$  What is the value of ${\\int }_{1}^{8}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{1}3g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-6 \\\\ 3{\\int }_{4}^{1}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-6 \\\\ -3{\\int }_{1}^{4}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-6 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{4}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{8}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{4}^{8}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+5 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
